<Base title="Start here: how buying a business works" description="A beginner’s orientation to acquisition entrepreneurship, EBITDA, valuation, debt, equity, seller financing, due diligence and ownership." type="Start here">
 <Crumbs items={[{title:'Start here'}]}/><header class="page-header"><p class="eyebrow">Your first pass through the subject</p><h1>An acquisition is a sequence of connected decisions.</h1><p>You can become an entrepreneur by starting a company or by buying an existing one. This guide follows the acquisition path: what to buy, what to pay, how to fund it, and what ownership requires.</p></header>
 <div class="split"><article>
 <section aria-labelledby="buy-vs-build-heading"><h2 id="buy-vs-build-heading">Two routes to entrepreneurship</h2><div class="related-columns spaced">
  <div><h3>Starting from scratch</h3><ul><li>Test the product and market, and find the first customers.</li><li>Build a team, processes and an operating record.</li><li>Plan for capital needs while working toward profitability.</li></ul></div>
  <div><h3>Buying an existing business</h3><ul><li>Examine existing customers, revenue and historical financials.</li><li>Take responsibility for employees, processes and obligations already in place.</li><li>Validate earnings and price; fund the purchase and manage repayments.</li><li>Plan the ownership transition and any integration work.</li></ul></div>
 </div><p class="spaced">Acquisition entrepreneurship changes the risks; it does not remove them. Valuation, leverage, diligence, management transition, integration and capital structure all need attention. Both paths require capital, judgment and operating work.</p></section>
 <ol class="step-list">
  <li><h2>Understand what changes hands.</h2><p>The opportunity includes customer relationships, employees, processes and trading history, along with dependencies and obligations. Existing profit is a starting point for investigation, not a guarantee of future results.</p><p>The source collection contrasts buying existing operations with building from scratch, while recording failures and circumstances where creating something new makes sense. <a href="/course/acquisition-entrepreneurship/">Understand the acquisition model.</a></p></li>
  <li><h2>Define a business you can responsibly own.</h2><p>A useful target combines a business model you understand, a manageable size, sustainable economics, and an operating team. A low asking multiple is not enough if the owner’s departure removes the company’s main salesperson or technical expert.</p><p><a href="/course/choosing-a-target/">Build a target profile.</a> Then <a href="/course/building-a-deal-pipeline/">learn how sourcing works.</a></p></li>
  <li><h2>Translate earnings into cash.</h2><p>EBITDA is earnings before interest, tax, depreciation, and amortization. It helps organize a discussion about operating earnings, but it is not the amount available to repay debt. Customers may pay late, equipment may need replacement, and tax and other cash demands remain.</p><p>Normalization asks whether the reported earnings reflect operations after the ownership change. If an owner worked without a salary, replacement management still needs to be paid. <a href="/course/earnings-versus-cash/">Read earnings versus cash.</a></p></li>
  <li><h2>Separate price from the payment structure.</h2><p>Enterprise value describes the value assigned to the operating business. Equity value concerns the shareholders’ interest after relevant debt, cash, and transaction adjustments. The source explanations simplify this bridge; a real deal requires agreed definitions.</p><p>Even after agreeing a price, the parties must decide how much is paid at closing, later, or only if a performance condition is met. A fixed deferred payment and an earn-out create different obligations. <a href="/course/structuring-the-price/">Explore payment structures.</a></p></li>
  <li><h2>Build the capital stack.</h2><p>A capital stack is the combination of money and claims used to fund a transaction. Debt has repayment obligations; equity exchanges capital for ownership and exposure to loss. Seller finance leaves part of the seller’s payment outstanding. Each affects cash needs, control, and risk.</p><p>The recurring practitioner sequence is to find a deal, establish debt capacity, then raise the equity gap. It is a framework with exceptions, including an explicitly proposed equity-first strategy. <a href="/course/building-the-capital-stack/">Follow the acquisition funding.</a></p></li>
  <li><h2>Verify before you complete.</h2><p>Due diligence tests the business and the transaction. Financial figures, customers, people, contracts, assets, and financing conditions all matter. A supportive lender conversation is not the same as an approved, unconditional transfer of funds.</p><p>Closing coordinates the documents, conditions, and release of consideration. The source includes fee deductions and last-minute trading changes that complicate apparently ready transactions. <a href="/course/diligencing-the-business/">Study diligence.</a> <a href="/course/getting-to-completion/">Understand closing.</a></p></li>
  <li><h2>Plan for ownership after the announcement.</h2><p>The repayment schedule continues after the acquisition closes. Someone must lead the staff, maintain customer relationships, report results, and manage cash. Delegating daily operations still requires an accountable management structure.</p><p><a href="/course/management-after-acquisition/">Learn how ownership and management fit together.</a></p></li>
 </ol><Sources ids={['IJIazMAuvmI','vmAmd5vmK8o','vkKw1MeUors','wd4Y23wruXU','akgHprIWcZU','byJSV1CEYBk','Vy-GvO4SGvI','NWQKr-gqNhc']} /></article>
